Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson (@gc-openstax: 0, is #reference, #part-of @gc-sources) { r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94
Paul Flowers, Klaus Theopold, Richard Langley, and William R. Robinson. Chemistry 2e. OpenStax/Rice University, 2019. English. [Publisher and authorized formats](https://openstax.org/details/books/chemistry-2e) · [Preface and current notices](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/preface) · [Start the book](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/1-introduction).

Read the complete two-semester textbook, including worked examples, chapter summaries, and substantial end-of-chapter problems. The Records assign the relevant chapters as the subject develops; later research is additional to this first whole-book foundation. Its organization covers quantitative chemistry, structure/bonding, gases and condensed matter, solutions, rates/equilibria, thermodynamics/electrochemistry, descriptive/organic chemistry, and nuclear chemistry.

The current publisher pages declare CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, with component-specific and trademark notices. Earlier retained editions may show different notices; identify the actual edition instead of inferring its terms from an old catalogue entry. This Record links the publisher's authorized work; no textbook text, figures, or PDF is bundled. Bibliography, scope, access, and current licence checked on 29 October 2026.

**Return:** [General chemistry sources — books, courses, data, and research](../Sources.lingua#r_37CHT3QSMD33JDMXE59S6M2ME3).

**Shared chemistry foundation — 31 October 2026:** selected publisher opening passages in [1.2, phases and classification](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/1-2-phases-and-classification-of-matter), [3.1, formula mass and amount](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/3-1-formula-mass-and-the-mole-concept), [4.1, writing and balancing equations](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-1-writing-and-balancing-chemical-equations), and [4.3, reaction stoichiometry](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-3-reaction-stoichiometry) were inspected. Study the introductory chapters and their elementary problems progressively with the shared Records. The assigned complete chapters and exercises were not fully audited. The added entity/fraction, transfer and symbolic-reaction investigations use independently written hypothetical accounts and independently checked arithmetic. No physical experiment or completed learner reading is claimed. The original whole-text assignment and access notices above remain applicable.


**Second chemistry-block inspection — 31 October 2026:** selected publisher passages were inspected in [6.1, electromagnetic quantities](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-1-electromagnetic-energy), [6.3, quantum descriptions and labels](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-3-development-of-quantum-theory), [6.4, configurations](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations), [6.5, periodic properties](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-5-periodic-variations-in-element-properties), [7.3, Lewis accounts](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures), [7.4, formal charge/resonance](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance), and [7.6, shape/polarity](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity). Inspection also covered selected opening/definition passages in [5.1, energy basics](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/5-1-energy-basics), [5.2, calorimetry](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/5-2-calorimetry), [5.3, first law/enthalpy](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/5-3-enthalpy), [10.1, interactions](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-1-intermolecular-forces), [10.3, phase transitions](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-3-phase-transitions), and [10.4, diagrams](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-4-phase-diagrams). The assigned sections/exercises are broader than these inspected passages; no complete chapter, exercise-set, simulation or newly inspected MIT/Portuguese-course audit is claimed. The common explanations distinguish arrow conventions, model-based charge assignments, orbital capacity versus filling, and phase energy from a chemical reaction. The stipulated spectral levels, vectors and material-X study are original teaching constructions, not measured properties or reproduced experiments. Original whole-text assignments and edition/component notices remain applicable, and all source media remain external.


**Third chemistry-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** rechecked the current [preface and CC BY-NC-SA 4.0/component notice](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/preface), and selected definitions/arguments in [12.1, reaction rates](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-1-chemical-reaction-rates), [12.3, rate laws](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-3-rate-laws), [12.4, integrated laws](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-4-integrated-rate-laws), [12.7, catalysis](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis), [13.1, dynamic equilibrium](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-1-chemical-equilibria), [13.2, quotients/constants](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-2-equilibrium-constants) and [13.3, perturbations](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-3-shifting-equilibria-le-chateliers-principle). Larger chapters/exercises remain assignments, not a fresh complete audit.

The common lessons distinguish signed net extent rates from nonnegative directional rates. They retain dimensionless activities and standard states rather than treating the text’s concentration shortcut as permission to ignore arbitrary unit changes. Worked mass-action expressions do not imply that every overall reaction has elementary kinetic orders. The reversible A/B and observation examples are original stipulated models, not adaptations of real reaction procedures or claims of experimental validation. Whole-source assignments and component notices remain unchanged; source media stay external.


**Fourth chemistry-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** selected official passages in [3.3 solution concentration and dilution](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/3-3-molarity), [11.1 dissolution](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-1-the-dissolution-process), [14.2 pH and water](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-2-ph-and-poh) and [14.3 acid/base equilibrium and approximations](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-3-relative-strengths-of-acids-and-bases) were inspected. The common lessons make the standard concentration, ideal activity approximation and water contribution explicit when interpreting introductory concentration formulas. Whole chapters/exercises and simulations remain assignments rather than claimed complete inspections or performed work. The original neutral-HA ledger and rational bracket investigation are new teaching constructions; no actual sample is prepared. Existing rights/component qualifications and readings remain, with media external.


**Fifth chemistry-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** freshly inspected selected opening redox/balancing passages in official section 17.1, electrode/external/ionic-path passages in 17.2, and quantitative charge/amount passages in 17.7. The original full-chapter assignments remain broader than this bounded inspection. The common derivation distinguishes dimensionless electron coefficient z from reaction extent ξ in mol, uses Q=∫I dt for varying current, and separately accounts for interfacial storage, competing reactions and product recovery. Its iron/copper/zinc ledger, inverse family, terminal record and programs are original hypothetical investigations, not reproductions of a source experiment. The official preface was rechecked for the current CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 notice; component exceptions and additional website-use conditions remain. Source figures/media are external.


**Sixth chemistry-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** the official preface again identifies the 2019 second edition and current CC BY-NC-SA 4.0/component notice. Selected [16.4](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-4-free-energy) and [17.4](https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-4-potential-free-energy-and-equilibrium) passages were inspected. Common lessons state the no-other-work premise behind Q=ΔH, distinguish finite ΔG from the reaction derivative, preserve dimensionless activity conventions and define the dimensionless electron coefficient separately from transferred amount. Example 16.7 was reconstructed from its supplied 298 K vaporization data: 8607.6 J/mol, consistent with 8.6 kJ/mol rounding. Example 17.8 using its rounded 0.0592 V coefficient gives 0.0206895121… V, consistent with 0.021 V. The concentration/activity and omitted-junction approximations remain explicit. These are bounded numerical reconstructions, not experiments, full chapter audits or reproductions of figures/media. Earlier assignments, edition notices and website conditions remain.

